{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001","id":"msg_011Cf5oLrDUAQtTsaFJdFrjW","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Perfect! The baseline tests all pass (13 tests). Now I'll provide my analysis:\n\n**Requirement, restated:** Add an HTTP POST endpoint to cancel orders, tracking order status as ACTIVE or CANCELLED, with idempotent cancellation semantics, all-or-nothing transactional behavior when shipments are CONFIRMED, and prevention of shipment creation on cancelled orders.\n\n**Repository findings:**\n- Order data class (no status field currently): `sample-service/src/main/kotlin/com/unityinflow/sample/order/Order.kt:11-16`\n- OrderRepository (in-memory ConcurrentHashMap): `sample-service/src/main/kotlin/com/unityinflow/sample/order/OrderRepository.kt`\n- OrderController (POST/GET handlers): `sample-service/src/main/kotlin/com/unityinflow/sample/order/OrderController.kt:15-46`\n- Shipment class (already has ShipmentStatus enum with CREATED/CONFIRMED/CANCELLED): `sample-service/src/main/kotlin/com/unityinflow/sample/shipment/Shipment.kt:9-27`\n- ShipmentRepository: `sample-service/src/main/kotlin/com/unityinflow/sample/shipment/ShipmentRepository.kt`\n- ShipmentController: `sample-service/src/main/kotlin/com/unityinflow/sample/shipment/ShipmentController.kt:20-56`\n- Error handling via ApiException hierarchy: `sample-service/src/main/kotlin/com/unityinflow/sample/api/ApiExceptions.kt`, `sample-service/src/main/kotlin/com/unityinflow/sample/api/ApiError.kt`\n- GlobalExceptionHandler: `sample-service/src/main/kotlin/com/unityinflow/sample/api/GlobalExceptionHandler.kt`\n\n**Conventions this repository has:**\n- Errors use ApiException subclasses (ConflictException for 409, ResourceNotFoundException for 404)\n- ErrorCode enum defines machine-readable error codes\n- All errors wrapped in ApiError envelope with code/message/fields\n- Tests verify both HTTP status AND error.code in JSON response\n- In-memory repositories with ConcurrentHashMap, no JPA/database\n- Kotlin data classes for domain models\n- Spring Boot 4.0.0, no external dependencies for domain logic\n\n**Affected files:**\n1. **Order.kt** — Add `status: OrderStatus` field (required to track order state)\n2. **OrderRepository.kt** — Already sufficient (save/findById work for status updates)\n3. **OrderController.kt** — Add `POST /orders/{orderId}/cancel` endpoint and inject ShipmentRepository for shipment queries\n4. **ShipmentRepository.kt** — Add `findByOrderId(orderId: String)` method (required to check shipment statuses during cancel)\n5. **ShipmentController.kt** — Add check to reject shipment creation for cancelled orders\n6. **ApiError.kt** — Add error codes for: order cannot be cancelled (CONFIRMED shipment exists), shipment creation on cancelled order\n7. **OrderControllerTest.kt** — Add tests for all cancel scenarios\n\n**Risks:**\n- Race condition: Check CONFIRMED shipment, then another thread confirms one → need atomic all-or-nothing semantics\n- Need to cancel CREATED shipments atomically with status update\n- Idempotency: Cancelling already-cancelled order must succeed without changes\n- Existing tests break if Order structure changes improperly\n\n**Open questions:**\n- Should OrderStatus be an enum (like ShipmentStatus)? Yes, for type safety and consistency.\n- What should cancel endpoint return? The updated Order (consistent with PUT/PATCH semantics).\n- Are there cascade/reference constraints to consider? No, repositories are independent, but business logic requires atomic checks.\n\n```\n<<PHASE:DESIGN>>\n```\n\n**Proposed changes, file by file:**\n\n1. **Order.kt** — Add `status: OrderStatus` enum with ACTIVE/CANCELLED states. New orders default to ACTIVE. Serializes/deserializes as JSON string.\n\n2. **OrderRepository.kt** — No changes needed; save() already updates the order with new status.\n\n3. **OrderController.kt** — Inject ShipmentRepository. Add POST `/orders/{orderId}/cancel` endpoint that:\n   - Throws 404 if order not found\n   - If order is already CANCELLED, return 200 with the order (idempotent success)\n   - Check all shipments for this order: if ANY is CONFIRMED, throw 409 ConflictException with new error code and revert nothing\n   - Otherwise, cancel all CREATED shipments and set order to CANCELLED, save both, return 200 with updated order\n\n4. **ShipmentRepository.kt** — Add `findByOrderId(orderId: String): List<Shipment>` method to find all shipments for an order.\n\n5. **ShipmentController.kt** — Before saving a shipment, check if the order is CANCELLED. If so, throw 409 ConflictException with new error code. Inject OrderRepository for this check.\n\n6. **ApiError.kt** — Add two error codes:\n   - `ORDER_SHIPMENT_CONFIRMED` — reject cancel if any shipment is CONFIRMED\n   - `ORDER_CANCELLED` — reject shipment creation on cancelled order\n\n7. **OrderControllerTest.kt** — Add tests:\n   - `cancels an active order and returns 200` — POST cancel, verify order is CANCELLED, verify returned order has status\n   - `idempotent cancel succeeds with no changes` — POST cancel twice, both return 200, second changes nothing\n   - `cancel fails with 409 if any shipment is CONFIRMED` — create shipment, confirm it, POST cancel, verify 409 and order still ACTIVE\n   - `cancel fails with 404 if order not found` — POST cancel for non-existent order\n   - `cancelled order also cancels all CREATED shipments` — create order, create multiple CREATED shipments, cancel order, verify shipments are CANCELLED\n\n**Alternative approaches rejected:**\n\n1. **Use String status instead of enum** — Rejected because: no type safety, prone to typos, ShipmentStatus is already an enum (consistency), and enum reflects domain semantics better.\n\n2. **Use transactions/database locks for atomicity** — Rejected because: service uses in-memory repositories, not JPA/database. Atomic semantics achieved through careful code ordering and exception handling.\n\n3. **Cancel shipment in a separate endpoint** — Rejected because: ticket requires cancelling shipments as side-effect of order cancellation; endpoint should be atomic.\n\n4. **Return 204 No Content instead of 200 with order** — Rejected because: ticket says \"cancels an active order... returns the order\", and convention is to return the updated state.\n\n**Data and error flow:**\n\n- **Happy path (cancel ACTIVE order)**: \n  1. POST /orders/O-1/cancel\n  2. Controller finds order (or throws 404)\n  3. If already CANCELLED, return 200 with order (idempotent)\n  4. Query all shipments for order: `findByOrderId(O-1)` \n  5. Check all shipments: if ANY status == CONFIRMED, throw 409 (ConflictException) with ORDER_SHIPMENT_CONFIRMED code, nothing changes\n  6. Otherwise, iterate shipments, mark CREATED ones as CANCELLED, save each\n  7. Mark order as CANCELLED, save order\n  8. Return 200 with updated order\n\n- **Cancel already-CANCELLED order**: \n  1. POST /orders/O-1/cancel\n  2. Controller finds order\n  3. Order.status == CANCELLED, return 200 with order unchanged\n\n- **Cancel blocked by CONFIRMED shipment**:\n  1. POST /orders/O-1/cancel\n  2. Controller finds order\n  3. Query shipments, find one with CONFIRMED status\n  4. Throw ConflictException(ORDER_SHIPMENT_CONFIRMED, message)\n  5. Order and all shipments unchanged\n  6. Client receives 409 with error code\n\n- **Create shipment on cancelled order**:\n  1. POST /shipments with orderId for cancelled order\n  2. ShipmentController injects OrderRepository\n  3. Before saving, check `orderRepository.findById(orderId)?.status == OrderStatus.CANCELLED`\n  4. If true, throw ConflictException(ORDER_CANCELLED, message)\n  5. Client receives 409 with error code\n\n**Test strategy:**\n\n1. Test successful active order cancellation: POST cancel, assert 200, assert order.status == CANCELLED, assert returned order has CANCELLED status\n2. Test idempotent cancellation: create order, POST cancel twice, both succeed, second request returns same state\n3. Test blocked by CONFIRMED shipment: create order, create shipment, confirm it (via repository), POST cancel, assert 409 and ORDER_SHIPMENT_CONFIRMED code, verify order still ACTIVE\n4. Test 404 on unknown order: POST cancel for non-existent order, assert 404 and ORDER_NOT_FOUND code\n5. Test CREATED shipments are cancelled: create order, create 2 CREATED shipments, cancel order, query shipments via repository, assert both are CANCELLED\n6. Test shipment creation blocked for cancelled order: create order, cancel it, POST /shipments for that order, assert 409 and ORDER_CANCELLED code\n7. Test new orders default to ACTIVE: create order, GET it, assert status == ACTIVE\n\nNo changes to existing test cases needed; all should still pass.\n\n```\n<<PHASE:IMPLEMENTATION>>\n```\n\nNow I'll implement the changes:"}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":8,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3559,"cache_read_input_tokens":20820,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":3559},"output_tokens":4,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"11f38fe1-4ea2-4fe2-b4a5-3f50f1782c4c","uuid":"22e8933f-64ae-4596-8a71-66d02d993d4f","timestamp":"2026-09-15T19:45:57.283Z","request_id":"req_011Cf5oLqjgmXiagWBNuJ8jS"}
